Add an Aerium Guard button to the address bar.

The three setup modes were reachable from two places, both of which have to be
gone looking for: the first-run screen, seen once and usually before anyone
knows what the tradeoffs mean, and chrome://settings/aeriumGuard. This adds a
third that is simply always there - a shield in the address bar that names the
mode currently in force and switches to another one in two clicks. It is the
same idea as Brave's shield button, minus the per-site part: Aerium's modes are
browser-wide, and the bubble says so rather than pretending otherwise.

Nothing about what a mode contains lives here
---------------------------------------------
The bubble calls aerium_presets::ApplyPreset() and aerium_presets::DetectPreset(),
the same two functions the settings page's handler and the first-run screen call,
from the same chrome/browser/ui/webui/aerium_presets.h. There is no second copy
of the pref list, and the prefs the button observes are exactly the fifteen named
in PresetValues, split between the profile PrefService and local state the way
that header splits them - getting that split wrong is a CHECK failure inside
PrefService, not a silent no-op.

The mode names in the bubble are the same messages the settings page shows,
IDS_SETTINGS_AERIUM_GUARD_*_LABEL. That is possible because settings_strings.grdp
is a <part> of generated_resources.grd, so everything in it ends up in the same
chrome/grit/generated_resources.h the browser UI reads. Wording, prefs and flags
therefore cannot drift between the two places by construction.

Off-the-record windows act on the profile they were opened from, not on the
incognito overlay, whose writes are discarded when the last such window closes.
chrome://settings behaves the same way, and a button that silently forgot what it
was told would be worse than no button.

Why this is not a page action
-----------------------------
At this revision the location bar has two page action frameworks side by side:
the legacy PageActionIconController, still holding the cookie controls, memory
saver, zoom and bookmark star icons, and the newer page_actions framework with
its per-tab PageActionController, ActionItem registration and
PageActionPropertiesProvider. Both exist because a migration is half done.

This button belongs to neither. A page action says something about the page in
the current tab; that is what the per-tab controllers, the ephemeral visibility
and the PageActionIconType-keyed CTR histograms are all built around. The Aerium
Guard button says nothing about the page. It is browser-wide and always shown, so
registering it as a page action would mean inventing a PageActionIconType and
threading it through IsPageActionMigrated(), the properties provider and the page
action histogram enums to buy behaviour it does not want.

Instead AeriumGuardIconView derives from PageActionIconView, which is what gives
an address-bar icon its size, insets, ink drop, focus behaviour and bubble
anchoring, and LocationBarView owns it directly, exactly as it already owns
IntentChipButton. It is laid out as one more trailing decoration, added after
both containers so it sits to their left and leaves the bookmark star as the
right-most thing in the bar, and its Update() is driven from
RefreshPageActionIconViews() so that it hides and reappears together with the
icons beside it while the user is typing in the omnibox.

The first BUILD.gn source list this patch set touches
----------------------------------------------------
Every earlier Aerium C++ addition was header-only, specifically so that no
BUILD.gn source list had to change. That trick does not work for a view: it needs
a .cc. So aerium_guard_icon_view.h joins the public list of
source_set("location_bar") and aerium_guard_icon_view.cc joins the sources of
source_set("impl"), in chrome/browser/ui/views/location_bar/BUILD.gn. Those are
the two halves of the target that already builds location_bar_view.{h,cc} and
star_view.{h,cc}, so the new file lands in the same translation unit set as the
code that instantiates it, with the same visibility and the same dependency set.

One dep is added, //components/vector_icons, for the shield glyph. Every other
header the new file includes is one that location_bar_view.cc or star_view.cc in
the same target already includes: browser_process.h, profile.h, the two
components/prefs headers, generated_resources.h and web_contents.h.
ui/base/mojom/dialog_button.mojom.h comes in through //ui/views, which lists
//ui/base/mojom in its public_deps. aerium_presets.h is in no target's source
list at all, so gn check has nothing to say about it, which is the same reason
settings_ui.cc can include it today.

The icon
--------
components/vector_icons/shield.icon exists at 151.0.7922.108 and is already in
the sources list of aggregate_vector_icons("components_vector_icons"), so it is
already generated into vector_icons::kShieldIcon and costs nothing to use. No new
.icon file, and no new entry in any icon list.

Strings
-------
Three new messages, in chrome/app/settings_strings.grdp beside the existing
Aerium Guard block: the button's tooltip, the bubble's one-line description, and
the line shown when the current settings match no mode. Everything else the
bubble displays - its title, the three mode names, the note about flags needing a
restart - reuses the messages the settings page already defines.
